james-will-3.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: james-will-3.logic.r1 and james-will-3.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; james-will-3.logic.r1.2 has "moral_world → indeterminism"; they differ when only "the world is consistently represented as a pluralism whose parts may affect one another through their conduct being either good or bad" is true, which the first allows
james-will-3.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps james-will-3.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "the world is consistently represented as a pluralism whose parts may affect one another through their conduct being either good or bad" is true and "the world is represented in the indeterministic way" is false. Is S2, "For the only consistent way of representing a pluralism and a world whose parts may affect one another through their conduct being either good or bad is the indeterministic way.", true then?
james-will-3.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: james-will-3.logic.r1 and james-will-3.logic.r1.3 have "moral_world → good_bad" and "genuine_poss → indeterminism"; james-will-3.logic.r1.2 has "moral_world"; they differ when only "acts are really good and bad", "there are real, genuine possibilities in the world" and "the world is represented in the indeterministic way" are true, which the first allows
james-will-3.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: james-will-3.logic.r1 and james-will-3.logic.r1.3 have "moral_world → indeterminism"; james-will-3.logic.r1.2 has "indeterminism"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
